\caption(Companion changes affect the training path while the deployed encoder/decoder remains observation-wise.)
\alttext(The same row wins against zero companions and loses against high companions; its ungated deployment endpoint is identical in both batches.)

Away from ties the gate is equivariant to permuting batch rows, but ties use the new row
indices. For an equal-score column with q=1, permuting row identities can therefore change
which original observation receives a slot. At a tied positive cutoff, an arbitrarily small
perturbation can replace one selected row by another, causing a finite jump in the gated
code. The frozen-mask derivative is useful on fixed support, not an everywhere smooth
derivative or a differentiability claim at ties.

> The executed training graph

The diagram runs an actual32-row batch, with four winners per feature. The shared decoder is
independent of the encoder and has a sigmoid output. Deployment bypasses the gate shown here.
Both variants own encoder/W,b and decoder/W,b:2×784×64+64+784=101,200 scalars.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wta_architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore p(1931);
  n::Module         model(
      [&](const n::Tensor& input) {
        const auto net = n::models::CompetitiveAutoencoder(p, input, true);
        n::model_stage("ReLU encoder 784→64", input, net.raw);
        n::model_stage("Lifetime rank / ceil(B/8) rows", net.raw, net.mask);
        n::model_stage(
            "Winner multiplication", std::vector<n::Tensor>{net.raw, net.mask}, net.training_code);
        return n::model_stage(
            "Independent sigmoid decoder", net.training_code, net.training_reconstruction);
      },
      "Lifetime WTA training graph: four batch winners per feature");
  n::ModelPlotOptions options;
  options.style                 = n::ModelPlotStyle::Graph;
  options.detail                = n::ModelPlotDetail::Blocks;
  options.max_neurons           = 8;
  options.sizing.spatial_scale  = n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;
  options.sizing.channel_scale  = n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;
  options.sizing.minimum_extent = 2;
  options.sizing.max_planes     = 6;
  const auto figure             = model.plot(n::zeros({32, 784}), options);
  if (p.count() != 101200 || p.size() != 4 || figure.parameter_count() != 101200)
    return 1;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  figure.save_svg("build/wta_architecture.svg");
  std::print(
      "{} learned tensors; {} learned scalars; selector is detached.\n", p.size(), p.count());
}
```
