// result_begin: markdown
| model | held-out MSE | zero-code MSE | shifted-code MSE | selected training-probe rows per feature | deployed nonzero fraction |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| competitive | 0.067503750 | 0.191458436 | 0.083673118 | 4 | 0.968516 |
| plain | 0.041842456 | 0.221799367 | 0.069317967 | 32 | 0.840313 |

Fitting-mean MSE 0.067798074. Fresh restore, exact encode/decode/repeat and immutable weights pass.
// result_end

The selected competitive MSE0.067503750 is only about0.43% below the fitting mean0.067798074,
and substantially above the plain model0.041842456. Competitive shifted/zero-code MSE is
0.083673118/0.191458436. Code alignment affects reconstruction, but this short run does not
show a reconstruction advantage from lifetime competition. No classification confusion matrix,
semantic disentanglement or general superiority follows from pixel MSE.

The competitive model's deployed test code is nonzero in96.85% of its12,800 coordinates,
versus84.03% for plain. The12.5% selected training-slot rate is therefore not its deployment
activity rate. This is an observed property of these selected weights and inputs, not a claim
that ReLU codes must always be dense.

> All ten digits without quality filtering

Columns are the first held-out occurrence of digits0–9, chosen without reconstruction scores.
Rows are original, competitive deployment, plain deployment, fitting mean, absolute competitive
error, zero-code decoder and shifted-code decoder. Every panel shares fixed[0,1] grayscale,
nearest-neighbour enlargement and unamplified errors. Deployment uses raw full ReLU codes,
with no copied source pixels and no training-mode gating in these reconstruction rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wta_reconstructions.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> data, sparse, plain;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz("build/wta_data.npz"))
    data.emplace(key, value);
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz("build/wta_competitive_outputs.npz"))
    sparse.emplace(key, value);
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz("build/wta_plain_outputs.npz"))
    plain.emplace(key, value);
  const auto mean                 = n::load_npz("build/wta_mean.npz")[0].second;
  const auto labels               = data.at("test_labels").to_vector<int>(),
             ids                  = data.at("test_source_ids").to_vector<int>();
  const auto                clean = data.at("test_images").reshape({200, 784});
  std::vector<std::int64_t> rows;
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit) {
    const auto found = std::find(labels.begin(), labels.end(), digit);
    if (found == labels.end())
      return 1;
    rows.push_back(found - labels.begin());
  }
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels;
  for (const auto& values : std::vector<n::Tensor>{
           clean,
           sparse.at("test_reconstruction"),
           plain.at("test_reconstruction"),
           mean,
           n::abs(sparse.at("test_reconstruction") - clean),
           sparse.at("zero_reconstruction"),
           sparse.at("shuffled_reconstruction")})
    panels.push_back(n::take_rows(values, rows));
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat(panels, 0).reshape({70, 28, 28, 1}),
      "build/wta_reconstructions.png",
      10,
      0,
      1,
      2,
      .8,
      3);
  std::print("| digit | original MNIST source ID |\n|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int digit = 0; digit < 10; ++digit)
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n", digit, ids[rows[digit]]);
}
