> Verification and limits

The core already owns stable signed selection and both competitive paths. This conversion uses
its lifetime mode directly; native Python/R retain their own loops and simple invariant checks.
Native tests add every small lifetime-model parameter derivative, input first/second derivatives
on positive separated support, batch-context counterexamples and zero-slot conventions.
Language tests retain owning endpoints after collection and distinguish16 positive deployment
activities from two selected training slots in an8×2 toy. All original29 archives are compared
by dtype, shape and value bytes, including first/partial/selected probes and all model snapshots.
The optional NumPy oracle checks full first derivatives, exact first Adam rounding, every
validation candidate, pool/control predictions and official disjoint source IDs.

These are fully connected educational fits with one seed and three epochs. Lifetime training
selection is batch-dependent and disappears at deployment; it does not guarantee per-image or
deployed sparsity. Ties are deterministic but not differentiable, and rounded selected slots
may contain zeros. Both paths retain dense64-coordinate storage; no codec, quantization or
bitrate benchmark is implemented. Blurry outputs, weak competitive MSE and the stronger plain
control remain visible rather than being treated as evidence for downstream usefulness.
